From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:27:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: trim page cache the host already caches

Every UML disk is a host file with a host page cache of its own, so a
guest page-cache page duplicates data the host already holds. Only the
guest's copy is charged to the emulator process: on iOS the host file
cache does not count against the application's memory limit at all.
Linux keeps page cache until memory runs out, so a file the guest read
once stayed resident at the application's expense for the rest of the
boot, and with guest RAM now returned on demand it is page cache that
keeps the footprint up.

Let the host have it back. While trimming is on, a deferrable worker
shrinks the inactive file pages above a floor by a quarter every two
seconds, through the same proactive reclaim memory.reclaim uses, without
swap. Freed pages reach the host through free-page reporting, and
reading one back is a copy from the host's cache. Reclaim takes the
coldest pages first and leaves writeback to the flusher.

A program that is starting reads files faster than it touches their
pages a second time, so a pass can take pages it is about to use.
Chromium's start-up read back about 20,000 file pages in 15 s, and
none without the trimmer, each a host read, on the EROFS root a
decompression, and for code a new translation. When more than a
quarter of what a pass reclaimed has been read back by the next pass,
the interval doubles, up to 32 times; a pass whose evictions stay out
restores it.

A guest in front of its user pays for every page it reads back, so
trimming is off until the host asks for it. The management console's
"trim on" and "trim off" start and stop it, as page_cache_trim.enabled
does on the command line and in sysfs. "trim once" is for a host about
to stop scheduling the guest. It takes the inactive file cache above
the floor for at most a second, compacts memory for at most another,
hands the guest's free memory to the host at once, and replies with the
amounts and the time each step took. The host takes memory back only
in whole chunks, and the pages reclaim frees lie between pages still in
use, so without compaction a pass released a third to a half of what
it freed. Whole-node compaction took from 0.7 to 5.6 s on a 4 GiB
Android guest, so compaction gains a deadline: compact_control.deadline
stops a compactor in __compact_finished(), and compact_nodes_until()
compacts every node as /proc/sys/vm/compact_memory does, up to one.
page_cache_trim.interval_ms and page_cache_trim.floor_mb tune trimming.
